1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Ready, aim, fire.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Eagle eyes and precision engineering on target and off the charts.

3
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
From the pool hall to the polar ice cap of Mars,

4
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
the quest for the ultimate inaccuracy.

5
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:31,000
Now, Bullseye on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:51,300
In warfare, the demanding and deadly challenge

7
00:00:51,400 --> 00:00:54,160
of extreme ballistic accuracy falls

8
00:00:54,260 --> 00:00:55,700
on the shoulders of the sniper.

9
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:00,740
His mission, pure and simple, is to hit the bullseye.

10
00:01:03,740 --> 00:01:04,940
Sniper ready?

11
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,040
Send it.

12
00:01:06,140 --> 00:01:10,440
Trainees learn the art, and experts hone it.

13
00:01:10,540 --> 00:01:12,340
Good to go.

14
00:01:12,440 --> 00:01:17,880
At the US Army Sniper School at Fort Benning, Georgia,

15
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:22,300
one of the most devastatingly accurate rifles they work with

16
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,000
requires a two-man team to operate effectively.

17
00:01:27,001 --> 00:01:30,000
The Barrett M107.

18
00:01:30,100 --> 00:01:34,600
The M107 fires magazines of 10, 50-caliber rounds.

19
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:37,640
To cushion the recoil and increase accuracy,

20
00:01:37,740 --> 00:01:40,240
the barrel assembly itself absorbs force

21
00:01:40,340 --> 00:01:42,980
as each bullet exits, moving inward

22
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:45,580
against large springs built into the receiver,

23
00:01:45,680 --> 00:01:48,960
the central mount that holds the firing mechanism.

24
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:54,600
Each round speeds toward the target at 2,850 feet per second.

25
00:01:54,601 --> 00:01:57,900
In this exercise, the challenge for Sergeant Anthony Palky

26
00:01:58,000 --> 00:02:00,040
and Staff Sergeant Rodney Medley

27
00:02:00,140 --> 00:02:01,240
Send one more.

28
00:02:01,340 --> 00:02:02,740
is to hit three separate bullseyes

29
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:07,540
from a range of over 400 yards.

30
00:02:07,640 --> 00:02:10,340
All right, give me left half a minute.

31
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:13,260
Medley, the spotter, uses a more powerful scope

32
00:02:13,360 --> 00:02:16,260
to determine the precise coordinates.

33
00:02:16,360 --> 00:02:19,360
Palky, the sniper, must adjust to those coordinates.

34
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:20,360
Left half a minute.

35
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:23,160
Send one more.

36
00:02:23,161 --> 00:02:26,060
to have a chance of hitting the bullseye.

37
00:02:26,160 --> 00:02:29,240
The M107's accuracy has one crucial variable working

38
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:33,500
against it at Fort Benning, the wind.

39
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,540
The wind conditions are real rough.

40
00:02:35,640 --> 00:02:38,640
From 300 to 500 meters, it's all open from here

41
00:02:38,740 --> 00:02:39,840
to 500 meters out.

42
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:42,540
So you've got the wind that just pushes from the right to left.

43
00:02:42,640 --> 00:02:44,040
All right, 12 o'clock, 4 inches.

44
00:02:44,140 --> 00:02:45,280
Send another.

45
00:02:45,380 --> 00:02:46,920
The bigger the bullet, the greater

46
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:49,780
the wind's effect on its flight.

47
00:02:49,781 --> 00:02:53,220
And the M107 fires a big armor-piercing incendiary

48
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:55,320
round.

49
00:02:55,420 --> 00:02:58,920
This one is a standard API round.

50
00:02:59,020 --> 00:03:01,620
And this will be your M107 round.

51
00:03:01,720 --> 00:03:03,420
The wind will push this bullet all over the place

52
00:03:03,520 --> 00:03:05,720
if you're not careful and know what you're doing with it.

53
00:03:05,820 --> 00:03:08,240
Given the wind, can the sniper team,

54
00:03:08,340 --> 00:03:10,540
using this precisely engineered weapon,

55
00:03:10,640 --> 00:03:15,440
hit three bullseyes more than a quarter mile in the distance?

56
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:16,940
Target three identified.

57
00:03:17,040 --> 00:03:18,840
437 meters.

58
00:03:18,841 --> 00:03:20,840
437, unduxed.

59
00:03:21,840 --> 00:03:23,840
Give me a half right.

60
00:03:23,841 --> 00:03:24,840
Half right.

61
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:26,840
And send it when you're ready.

62
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:29,840
Ready.

63
00:03:29,841 --> 00:03:30,840
Send it.

64
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:39,840
Move to the right, V target.

65
00:03:44,840 --> 00:03:46,840
Target on the left, it's the shorter one.

66
00:03:46,841 --> 00:03:47,880
It's the shorter one.

67
00:03:47,881 --> 00:03:49,000
Roger, got shorty.

68
00:03:56,680 --> 00:04:00,380
All right, let's see what happened to the target.

69
00:04:00,381 --> 00:04:04,360
Yes, the M107, devastating.

70
00:04:04,361 --> 00:04:06,200
Punches holes through the target.

71
00:04:06,201 --> 00:04:08,960
This is a one-inch Iron Maiden.

72
00:04:08,961 --> 00:04:11,040
And what this round has done, not only has it punched a hole,

73
00:04:11,041 --> 00:04:13,740
but it's melted a hole through this target.

74
00:04:13,741 --> 00:04:15,240
That's why we call it the API,

75
00:04:15,241 --> 00:04:18,080
the armor-piercing incendiary round.

76
00:04:18,081 --> 00:04:20,540
We hit it here in the chest, over here on the side,

77
00:04:20,541 --> 00:04:22,340
and up in the head.

78
00:04:22,341 --> 00:04:24,540
Yeah, that person's not having a good day.

79
00:04:24,541 --> 00:04:26,480
This other littler target, well,

80
00:04:26,481 --> 00:04:29,120
looks like it was blown off of its braces.

81
00:04:29,121 --> 00:04:32,560
We got a solid hole, and then we got a glancing blow there

82
00:04:32,561 --> 00:04:33,760
after it fell down.

83
00:04:33,761 --> 00:04:36,200
And if anyone was driving this vehicle,

84
00:04:36,201 --> 00:04:40,140
as this poor sucker was, you can see here

85
00:04:40,141 --> 00:04:41,500
where all the black marks are.

86
00:04:41,501 --> 00:04:43,440
These are holes that have been punched through.

87
00:04:43,441 --> 00:04:48,180
devastating effect of the M107 with an API round.

88
00:04:48,181 --> 00:04:51,320
Once they dial in, these guys can hit bullseyes a quarter

89
00:04:51,321 --> 00:04:53,660
mile away all day.

90
00:04:53,661 --> 00:04:57,320
But how about a tank from a range of over half a mile?

91
00:04:57,321 --> 00:05:00,760
They recalibrate for just below the tank's turret.

92
00:05:00,761 --> 00:05:02,460
Send it when you're ready.

93
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:05,820
Bullseye.

94
00:05:05,821 --> 00:05:16,560
Hyper-accurate rifles like the M107 can trace their origins

95
00:05:16,660 --> 00:05:18,560
to some pioneering weapons of the past.

96
00:05:18,660 --> 00:05:24,780
The first, says US Army historian David Stushan,

97
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:29,380
is the Pennsylvania flintlock from the 1700s.

98
00:05:29,480 --> 00:05:33,680
This is a replica of about a 1760 flintlock Pennsylvania

99
00:05:33,780 --> 00:05:34,680
rifle.

100
00:05:34,681 --> 00:05:39,680
This is a .54 caliber solid soft lead rifle ball.

101
00:05:39,780 --> 00:05:41,020
And it's just like the ones

102
00:05:41,021 --> 00:05:43,980
that the less accurate smoothbore muskets fired,

103
00:05:43,981 --> 00:05:47,900
but the difference is that it's wrapped in a greased patch.

104
00:05:47,901 --> 00:05:51,600
This loading block has got balls already patched

105
00:05:51,601 --> 00:05:55,400
and greased with sheep tallow and beeswax,

106
00:05:55,401 --> 00:05:57,780
which allows it to load very tightly

107
00:05:57,781 --> 00:05:59,400
in the inside of the barrel.

108
00:05:59,401 --> 00:06:02,200
So when it's fired, the bullet is spun

109
00:06:02,201 --> 00:06:06,000
by the twisted grooves on the inside of the rifle.

110
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:08,880
What that gives you is greater accuracy and range.

111
00:06:08,881 --> 00:06:14,920
You can shoot a deer or a redcoat at 300 yards.

112
00:06:14,921 --> 00:06:17,240
In its time, the Pennsylvania flintlock rifle

113
00:06:17,241 --> 00:06:19,960
was the world's most accurate firearm.

114
00:06:19,961 --> 00:06:22,320
But just how accurate was that?

115
00:06:22,321 --> 00:06:25,960
Deve's target is 25 yards away.

116
00:06:25,961 --> 00:06:27,200
X marks the bullseye.

117
00:06:27,201 --> 00:06:34,900
So the old hunters and marksmen would look where they were aiming and look where the bullet

118
00:06:34,901 --> 00:06:40,760
went, and they would measure to the opposite direction to compensate by aiming here so

119
00:06:40,761 --> 00:06:42,440
the bullet would hit here.

120
00:06:42,441 --> 00:06:44,980
So this is called Kentucky windage.

121
00:06:44,981 --> 00:06:50,900
Let's try it again.

122
00:06:50,901 --> 00:06:56,520
Not a bullseye, but plenty close enough to put venison on the table.

123
00:06:56,521 --> 00:07:01,520
In the mid-19th century, the Springfield Model 1842

124
00:07:01,620 --> 00:07:04,460
embodied three new advancements in rifle technology

125
00:07:04,560 --> 00:07:05,960
that made for greater accuracy.

126
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:11,560
First was a new bullet, the mini ball.

127
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:14,580
The difference is it's shaped sort of like a football,

128
00:07:14,680 --> 00:07:16,480
and the base of it is hollow.

129
00:07:16,580 --> 00:07:18,180
It has a cavity or cone.

130
00:07:18,280 --> 00:07:22,120
When it's fired, the powder explodes into this cavity,

131
00:07:22,220 --> 00:07:26,520
making a tight fit or seal inside the barrel.

132
00:07:26,521 --> 00:07:29,320
This paired with its more aerodynamic shape

133
00:07:29,321 --> 00:07:32,360
maximized both its accuracy and range.

134
00:07:32,361 --> 00:07:35,560
So instead of having a 300-yard accuracy,

135
00:07:35,561 --> 00:07:38,660
now it's 750 yards.

136
00:07:38,661 --> 00:07:40,200
The Springfield's second advancement

137
00:07:40,201 --> 00:07:44,000
that increased accuracy was an adjustable sight.

138
00:07:44,001 --> 00:07:46,300
It's graduated to every 100 yards

139
00:07:46,301 --> 00:07:49,780
all the way up to 900 yards.

140
00:07:49,781 --> 00:07:51,780
The third improvement was a percussion cap

141
00:07:51,781 --> 00:07:54,820
that ignited the main charge instead of the flint and steel

142
00:07:54,821 --> 00:07:58,460
mechanism used in the flintlock. Not only is this more reliable,

143
00:07:58,860 --> 00:08:02,080
but it means that the marksman can have his eyes open when he's

144
00:08:02,081 --> 00:08:05,100
pulling the trigger and not have all those tiny pieces of flint and

145
00:08:05,101 --> 00:08:07,680
that exploding powder in his face as a distraction.

146
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:12,500
From the same 25 yards, Dave aims again for the bullseye.

147
00:08:13,320 --> 00:08:16,020
Is the Springfield indeed more accurate than the flintlock of a

148
00:08:16,021 --> 00:08:22,160
century earlier? The experts say absolutely,

149
00:08:22,161 --> 00:08:27,360
but Dave's still slightly off. This next rifle should help him

150
00:08:27,361 --> 00:08:32,800
out, the rugged Russian-made Mosin Nagant, first produced

151
00:08:32,801 --> 00:08:36,800
in 1891. It served in various modified forms

152
00:08:36,801 --> 00:08:39,920
for 60 years and became the weapon of choice for Soviet

153
00:08:39,921 --> 00:08:44,880
snipers. Breach loading improved accuracy

154
00:08:44,881 --> 00:08:48,480
because now bullets could be made larger than the barrel and be

155
00:08:48,481 --> 00:08:52,160
forced in tightly from the breach rather than ramming.

156
00:08:52,161 --> 00:08:56,160
through the barrel. When equipped with a telescopic

157
00:08:56,260 --> 00:09:00,100
sight, its accuracy was fearsome.

158
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,200
Can Dave finally hit the bullseye using the more

159
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:05,100
advanced rifle?

160
00:09:08,400 --> 00:09:11,520
All in all, that's not bad shooting with these old guns.

161
00:09:11,620 --> 00:09:15,120
But snipers don't shoot at paper.

162
00:09:15,220 --> 00:09:17,120
They shoot watermelons.

163
00:09:17,121 --> 00:09:21,620
Collins.

164
00:09:21,621 --> 00:09:26,320
It's one thing to make a bullseye out of a watermelon.

165
00:09:26,321 --> 00:09:29,360
When you're a cop, the bullseye can fire back.

166
00:09:32,720 --> 00:09:35,520
Police departments, including the LAPD,

167
00:09:35,521 --> 00:09:38,220
have tried to break down fast, accurate, and responsible

168
00:09:38,221 --> 00:09:41,420
shooting to a science.

169
00:09:41,421 --> 00:09:43,360
In our presentation to the target,

170
00:09:43,361 --> 00:09:45,580
we've broken it down in our teachings

171
00:09:45,581 --> 00:09:49,320
to a five-count presentation, much like a dance step.

172
00:09:49,321 --> 00:09:51,960
Our first count is to maintain our shooting grip

173
00:09:51,961 --> 00:09:53,680
and break the safety on the holster.

174
00:09:53,681 --> 00:09:56,760
Our second count is to what we call close contact.

175
00:09:56,761 --> 00:09:59,700
Third would be to a low ready.

176
00:09:59,701 --> 00:10:01,700
Count four, align the sights.

177
00:10:01,701 --> 00:10:04,000
And then on count five, it actually press the trigger.

178
00:10:10,080 --> 00:10:12,540
Rick Bennett has won four world championships

179
00:10:12,541 --> 00:10:15,580
in team police pistol competitions.

180
00:10:15,581 --> 00:10:18,320
A standard target practice distance for Rick and all

181
00:10:18,420 --> 00:10:22,880
police officers is just seven yards, as most shootouts occur

182
00:10:22,980 --> 00:10:26,920
within that narrow range. What I'm going to do now is

183
00:10:27,020 --> 00:10:30,320
post a competition target, and I'm going to go ahead and shoot

184
00:10:30,420 --> 00:10:33,960
that at 25 yards, which would be a distance that we would engage

185
00:10:34,060 --> 00:10:38,700
in competition. Rick's competition pistol

186
00:10:38,800 --> 00:10:42,200
isn't standard police issue. It's a Colt model 1911

187
00:10:42,201 --> 00:10:46,200
chambered in 9mm, and the goal here is to place all six rounds

188
00:10:46,201 --> 00:10:48,200
right into the ten ring.

189
00:10:55,200 --> 00:10:57,200
I'm going to go ahead and give you six more.

190
00:11:08,200 --> 00:11:10,200
You want six more?

191
00:11:10,201 --> 00:11:15,000
We're convinced. Eons before ballistics,

192
00:11:15,001 --> 00:11:18,500
primitive man began our long journey in search of the bullseye

193
00:11:18,501 --> 00:11:23,540
with a spear. About 40,000 years ago,

194
00:11:23,541 --> 00:11:28,460
a dart-throwing weapon called the atlatl followed, which used

195
00:11:28,461 --> 00:11:32,460
leverage to improve upon the spear's range and accuracy.

196
00:11:32,461 --> 00:11:39,460
Then 10,000 years ago, the earliest bow and arrow made its

197
00:11:39,461 --> 00:11:43,460
mark. Until the advent of firearms,

198
00:11:43,461 --> 00:11:47,460
it endured as the most accurate weapon on Earth.

199
00:11:47,461 --> 00:11:51,460
Today, its high-tech, hyper-accurate incarnation is the

200
00:11:51,461 --> 00:11:56,460
compound bow. Invented for hunting, it's also

201
00:11:56,461 --> 00:12:01,460
used in archery competitions.

202
00:12:01,461 --> 00:12:04,700
Unlike traditional bows, it has extra stiff limbs

203
00:12:04,800 --> 00:12:06,800
made of composite materials, which

204
00:12:06,900 --> 00:12:10,900
must be bent using a levering system of cables and pulleys.

205
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,700
The key to its accuracy, when fully drawn,

206
00:12:13,800 --> 00:12:16,700
the pulleys, not the archer, hold part or even most

207
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:20,120
of the draw weight. This allows the archer to draw and aim

208
00:12:20,220 --> 00:12:21,820
the bow with much less strain.

209
00:12:24,920 --> 00:12:28,120
Levi Morgan and Samantha Klein are the Archery Shooters

210
00:12:28,220 --> 00:12:31,060
Association's top-ranked male and female

211
00:12:31,061 --> 00:12:34,560
shooters for 2008. We challenged them to hit the

212
00:12:34,561 --> 00:12:37,560
bullseye on the foam targets in the distance.

213
00:12:37,561 --> 00:12:40,400
That's the main thing in our competition, unknown distance.

214
00:12:40,401 --> 00:12:42,900
And that's what we practice more than shooting or anything.

215
00:12:42,901 --> 00:12:46,400
We practice judging yardage. For every yard Levi and Sam

216
00:12:46,401 --> 00:12:49,520
misjudge their range, their arrows will strike one inch,

217
00:12:49,521 --> 00:12:54,220
high or low, off the bullseye etched on the target's side.

218
00:12:54,221 --> 00:12:58,860
Nowadays, we shoot a scope with a slide bar where we sight in

219
00:12:58,861 --> 00:13:02,760
our own marks, and you just move it to how far you judge

220
00:13:02,860 --> 00:13:05,660
the target for. They estimate the target's

221
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:09,300
range at 42 yards, then calibrate their scopes

222
00:13:09,400 --> 00:13:12,300
accordingly. The challenge now is to take

223
00:13:12,400 --> 00:13:16,180
perfect aim, using not only the scope housing, but also the

224
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:19,320
peephole mounted on the bowstring. I'm sighting from

225
00:13:19,420 --> 00:13:21,920
the peep to my scope. You want everything to center

226
00:13:22,020 --> 00:13:24,160
up on that green fiber. That has to be in the center

227
00:13:24,260 --> 00:13:25,920
of your peep or you're not going to hit where you want to

228
00:13:26,020 --> 00:13:27,660
hit. When you come full draw, you

229
00:13:27,661 --> 00:13:30,560
the size of the opening in your peat to match up perfectly

230
00:13:30,660 --> 00:13:33,100
with your scope housing. If you have a bigger hole like this,

231
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:34,660
it's going to be able to move around in it.

232
00:13:34,760 --> 00:13:36,660
You want the same size so it lines up perfectly.

233
00:13:36,760 --> 00:13:38,700
A lot of people think it's just a matter of pulling back

234
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:41,300
and letting it go, but something little like that

235
00:13:41,400 --> 00:13:43,700
can change it three or four inches.

236
00:13:43,800 --> 00:13:48,220
Levi targets the wild boar, Samantha the deer.

237
00:13:48,320 --> 00:13:52,120
Is their estimate of the range correct?

238
00:13:52,220 --> 00:13:54,220
The only way to find out is to let fly.

239
00:13:57,660 --> 00:14:04,660
And fly some more.

240
00:14:17,020 --> 00:14:20,020
Bullseye precision, worthy of Robin Hood.

241
00:14:20,021 --> 00:14:25,020
Can these guys be just as accurate with medieval heavy

242
00:14:25,021 --> 00:14:27,660
be just as accurate with medieval heavy artillery?

243
00:14:27,760 --> 00:14:28,660
Fire in the hole.

244
00:14:35,160 --> 00:14:40,360
An SM-3 missile rises from its sleep on a Navy warship.

245
00:14:40,460 --> 00:14:43,880
It climbs at over 5,000 miles per hour.

246
00:14:43,980 --> 00:14:47,780
Its mission sounds like something out of science fiction,

247
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:51,220
to strike and destroy a US satellite falling towards Earth

248
00:14:51,320 --> 00:14:55,020
at 17,000 miles per hour.

249
00:14:55,021 --> 00:15:01,260
If it succeeds, it will be like a bullet hitting a bullet.

250
00:15:01,261 --> 00:15:03,260
Bullseye.

251
00:15:03,261 --> 00:15:06,760
Astonishing accuracy like this is the culmination of advances

252
00:15:06,761 --> 00:15:11,260
by weapons makers dating back to antiquity.

253
00:15:11,261 --> 00:15:17,380
George Metz is an expert on the ballista, a torsion-powered

254
00:15:17,381 --> 00:15:20,820
projectile launcher first used by the Greeks in the 5th century

255
00:15:20,821 --> 00:15:24,920
in the 5th century BC and later by the Romans.

256
00:15:24,921 --> 00:15:28,560
It fired a short, heavy arrow called a bolt.

257
00:15:28,561 --> 00:15:30,060
I would just look down the barrel,

258
00:15:30,061 --> 00:15:32,460
down the slider, which is like the barrel of a rifle,

259
00:15:32,461 --> 00:15:34,760
where I feel it's going to go, and after a few trial shots,

260
00:15:34,761 --> 00:15:36,260
I'm probably going to be on the mark.

261
00:15:36,261 --> 00:15:37,980
This is an anti-personnel weapon.

262
00:15:37,981 --> 00:15:40,980
It's to fire through an enemy's shield or into his body

263
00:15:40,981 --> 00:15:44,920
at a range of 100, 150 feet with accuracy.

264
00:15:44,921 --> 00:15:47,920
The accuracy of the ballista was limited, however,

265
00:15:47,921 --> 00:15:51,920
as its engineering focused more on power than precision.

266
00:15:51,921 --> 00:15:56,160
But could a skilled warrior overcome its design deficiencies?

267
00:15:56,161 --> 00:15:58,960
George's target is 50 yards away.

268
00:15:58,961 --> 00:16:00,800
It can hit that, yes.

269
00:16:00,801 --> 00:16:05,680
One, two, three.

270
00:16:05,681 --> 00:16:09,680
George compensates by aiming higher.

271
00:16:09,681 --> 00:16:10,880
No more elevation now.

272
00:16:15,280 --> 00:16:17,280
No rollman would give up now,

273
00:16:17,281 --> 00:16:18,180
And neither does George.

274
00:16:25,580 --> 00:16:28,020
In the Middle Ages, the search for a more consistently

275
00:16:28,021 --> 00:16:29,820
accurate projectile-launching weapon

276
00:16:29,821 --> 00:16:33,120
culminated with the trebuchet.

277
00:16:33,121 --> 00:16:37,040
Designed to pulverize the walls of a city or castle under siege,

278
00:16:37,041 --> 00:16:42,180
it dominated the landscape of war for over 400 years.

279
00:16:42,181 --> 00:16:46,140
This trebuchet is the handiwork of Grig Mullen and Wayne Neal,

280
00:16:46,141 --> 00:16:48,540
at Virginia Military Institute.

281
00:16:48,640 --> 00:16:49,880
That's got it.

282
00:16:49,980 --> 00:16:52,240
Fire in the hole.

283
00:16:52,340 --> 00:16:55,380
As a siege weapon, it didn't matter if you were hitting

284
00:16:55,480 --> 00:16:58,080
an exact place on the wall as long as every time you fired,

285
00:16:58,180 --> 00:16:59,480
you hit the same place.

286
00:16:59,580 --> 00:17:01,480
It didn't matter if you were here or here,

287
00:17:01,580 --> 00:17:04,500
as long as every time you fired, you hit here.

288
00:17:04,600 --> 00:17:08,200
A trebuchet works like a seesaw, using leverage.

289
00:17:08,300 --> 00:17:12,100
On one end of a pivoted beam is a sling holding a projectile.

290
00:17:12,200 --> 00:17:14,740
On the other end is a counterweight.

291
00:17:14,741 --> 00:17:18,040
When the counterweight falls, the beam and sling swing up

292
00:17:18,140 --> 00:17:21,540
to a vertical position, where one end of the sling releases,

293
00:17:21,640 --> 00:17:23,780
propelling the projectile towards the target

294
00:17:23,880 --> 00:17:24,780
with great force.

295
00:17:26,980 --> 00:17:28,380
And then by adjusting the sling,

296
00:17:28,480 --> 00:17:31,920
they'd hit the top of the wall, start to break the wall down,

297
00:17:32,020 --> 00:17:33,400
and then adjust the sling length,

298
00:17:33,500 --> 00:17:35,360
and just walk the projectiles down the wall

299
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:38,460
and knock chunks out of it so that the troops could go in.

300
00:17:38,560 --> 00:17:40,340
We challenged the professors to prove

301
00:17:40,440 --> 00:17:43,800
their trebuchet's accuracy by battering this 10-foot by 10-foot

302
00:17:43,801 --> 00:17:47,800
target 75 yards away. The first projectiles in their

303
00:17:47,801 --> 00:17:51,800
arsenal, watermelons. Three, two, one, fire in the

304
00:17:51,801 --> 00:17:56,800
hole. Two variables come into play when

305
00:17:56,801 --> 00:18:01,800
adjusting the trebuchet's aim, the weight of the projectile and

306
00:18:01,801 --> 00:18:05,800
the length of the sling. We're lengthening the sling to

307
00:18:05,801 --> 00:18:08,800
make the same weight projectile. We think we've got another

308
00:18:08,801 --> 00:18:12,800
watermelon that's about the same weight.

309
00:18:12,801 --> 00:18:14,760
release later.

310
00:18:14,761 --> 00:18:16,140
We were going over the target.

311
00:18:16,141 --> 00:18:18,640
By releasing later, it'll be a flatter trajectory

312
00:18:18,641 --> 00:18:21,040
and should be more of a line drive into the target

313
00:18:21,041 --> 00:18:22,840
as opposed to a mortar shot over it.

314
00:18:22,841 --> 00:18:24,340
Fire in the hole.

315
00:18:24,341 --> 00:18:25,840
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

316
00:18:25,841 --> 00:18:28,140
Come on, come on, come on, come on.

317
00:18:28,141 --> 00:18:29,220
Yes!

318
00:18:29,221 --> 00:18:31,260
Yeah, you can see watermelon pieces all over.

319
00:18:31,261 --> 00:18:35,320
And that's half-inch plywood, which is not negligible.

320
00:18:35,321 --> 00:18:38,960
And so a 15-pound watermelon at 80 miles an hour

321
00:18:38,961 --> 00:18:40,800
pretty well punched through that.

322
00:18:40,801 --> 00:18:43,740
Normally, they want to hit the wall perpendicular anyway

323
00:18:43,840 --> 00:18:46,540
and not come down on the wall.

324
00:18:46,640 --> 00:18:48,140
Next, we challenge the professors

325
00:18:48,240 --> 00:18:52,440
to pummel the bullseye with a slightly harder projectile,

326
00:18:52,540 --> 00:18:53,840
a 10-pound bowling ball.

327
00:18:59,860 --> 00:19:01,360
Like they did for the watermelon,

328
00:19:01,460 --> 00:19:03,160
they'll have to make adjustments to account

329
00:19:03,260 --> 00:19:05,600
for the weight of the bowling ball.

330
00:19:05,700 --> 00:19:08,300
We need to shorten the sling so it releases and goes higher.

331
00:19:08,400 --> 00:19:10,100
And we started out positioning the ball right

332
00:19:10,101 --> 00:19:13,140
on the center of the runway, and now we're shifting it.

333
00:19:13,240 --> 00:19:16,340
Basically, the plane of the sling perpendicular

334
00:19:16,440 --> 00:19:18,340
to the axis is about right here.

335
00:19:18,440 --> 00:19:21,640
So what we've done is shift the plane this way

336
00:19:21,740 --> 00:19:25,040
to get us to where we're throwing slightly to the left.

337
00:19:25,140 --> 00:19:27,160
You can do a little bit of adjusting,

338
00:19:27,260 --> 00:19:28,760
other than moving this whole thing.

339
00:19:28,860 --> 00:19:30,960
Yeah.

340
00:19:31,060 --> 00:19:33,860
Now can they hit the bullseye?

341
00:19:33,960 --> 00:19:35,860
Some brick.

342
00:19:35,960 --> 00:19:37,100
We've got distance.

343
00:19:37,200 --> 00:19:40,100
If this was a true siege, we'd just knock it out.

344
00:19:40,101 --> 00:19:43,100
this section of the wall instead of this section of the wall.

345
00:19:43,101 --> 00:19:45,740
We can try shifting it even further to the left,

346
00:19:45,741 --> 00:19:50,100
or we can move the wall over.

347
00:19:50,101 --> 00:19:52,100
That's an expedient option medieval trebuchet

348
00:19:52,101 --> 00:19:54,100
operators didn't have.

349
00:19:54,101 --> 00:19:56,100
But it's easier to shift a wooden wall

350
00:19:56,101 --> 00:20:00,100
than a 4,000-pound trebuchet.

351
00:20:00,101 --> 00:20:02,100
Grieg also pounds in stakes to keep track

352
00:20:02,101 --> 00:20:09,100
of where the rounds have hit.

353
00:20:09,101 --> 00:20:12,100
We moved the wall and now we hit where the wall used to be.

354
00:20:12,101 --> 00:20:16,100
We need to shift to the right some.

355
00:20:16,101 --> 00:20:22,100
What we've figured out in the years operating these things is that from the historical record,

356
00:20:22,101 --> 00:20:25,100
we've got 95% of the story.

357
00:20:25,101 --> 00:20:30,100
What we're missing is the last 5% that's the tricks of the trades for a trebuchet operator.

358
00:20:30,101 --> 00:20:33,100
And the last one of those guys died 500 years ago.

359
00:20:33,101 --> 00:20:36,100
When trebuchets went out of use, that knowledge died.

360
00:20:36,101 --> 00:20:38,100
And so the only way we can revive that

361
00:20:38,200 --> 00:20:40,340
is what we're doing now is practice.

362
00:20:40,440 --> 00:20:42,940
And so we keep trying.

363
00:20:43,040 --> 00:20:44,740
And trying.

364
00:20:44,840 --> 00:20:45,540
And trying.

365
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:51,240
And trying again.

366
00:20:51,340 --> 00:20:54,560
Oh, ha, ha, ha, ha.

367
00:20:54,660 --> 00:20:55,960
Smelled burnt rubber.

368
00:20:56,060 --> 00:20:58,960
It hit the wall first, and the friction burned the rubber

369
00:20:59,060 --> 00:21:00,260
off the outside of the ball.

370
00:21:00,360 --> 00:21:03,400
It landed right there.

371
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:05,200
Knocked the stake out that was holding the center of the wall

372
00:21:05,201 --> 00:21:07,240
and broke the two by four, and we've ruptured the plywood

373
00:21:07,340 --> 00:21:08,340
right there.

374
00:21:10,380 --> 00:21:13,240
Although Grigg and Wayne never hit the bullseye per se,

375
00:21:13,340 --> 00:21:15,480
they think their aim would have been more than good enough

376
00:21:15,580 --> 00:21:18,680
for a medieval commander.

377
00:21:18,780 --> 00:21:20,880
When we pull the wall out of the way,

378
00:21:20,980 --> 00:21:23,320
you see the stakes where we staked every time we've hit.

379
00:21:23,420 --> 00:21:25,680
You'll see the pattern that we're just right there.

380
00:21:25,780 --> 00:21:28,720
If this was a 20-foot high wall, we'd have pounded the crud

381
00:21:28,820 --> 00:21:30,760
out of it by now.

382
00:21:30,860 --> 00:21:33,000
By the 14th century, the trebuchet

383
00:21:33,001 --> 00:21:37,040
was being eclipsed by a terrifying new long-range weapon,

384
00:21:37,140 --> 00:21:40,040
the cannon.

385
00:21:40,140 --> 00:21:44,540
But for 500 years, all cannons had smoothbore barrels,

386
00:21:44,640 --> 00:21:47,640
in which the projectiles fit loosely, making them clumsy

387
00:21:47,740 --> 00:21:48,740
at finding the bullseye.

388
00:21:53,460 --> 00:21:57,260
But the bullseye finally came within reach in the mid-1800s

389
00:21:57,360 --> 00:21:58,760
and America's civil war.

390
00:21:58,761 --> 00:22:04,760
The rifled boar put a new spin on every round fired,

391
00:22:04,860 --> 00:22:07,760
revolutionizing the cannon just as it had the musket.

392
00:22:10,840 --> 00:22:12,800
This gave them tremendous accuracy

393
00:22:12,900 --> 00:22:14,680
and much harder hitting power.

394
00:22:14,780 --> 00:22:17,340
It also meant that they could now shoot a cannonball

395
00:22:17,440 --> 00:22:19,740
further than the gunners or cannoneers could see.

396
00:22:23,280 --> 00:22:26,280
By World War I, the cannon's longer accurate range

397
00:22:26,281 --> 00:22:29,320
had compelled artillery experts to develop innovative new

398
00:22:29,420 --> 00:22:30,420
tactics.

399
00:22:30,520 --> 00:22:33,760
How do you fire over the horizon several miles

400
00:22:33,860 --> 00:22:37,400
to an enemy target that the cannon cannot even see?

401
00:22:37,500 --> 00:22:39,060
And so now you're working with maps.

402
00:22:39,160 --> 00:22:41,300
You're working with graphs and grid lines

403
00:22:41,400 --> 00:22:42,560
and this type of thing.

404
00:22:42,660 --> 00:22:44,700
You have to have communication from the soldiers

405
00:22:44,800 --> 00:22:48,040
who can see the enemy target and tell the cannons where

406
00:22:48,140 --> 00:22:51,240
to shoot and how to adjust their rounds onto the target

407
00:22:51,340 --> 00:22:53,400
to make it more accurate to hit the bullseye.

408
00:22:56,280 --> 00:22:59,040
By the 1940s, artillery experts

409
00:22:59,140 --> 00:23:01,580
redefined the parameters of hitting the bullseye

410
00:23:01,680 --> 00:23:04,320
with a proximity fuse.

411
00:23:04,420 --> 00:23:06,120
Projectiles so equipped no longer

412
00:23:06,220 --> 00:23:08,220
had to hit the target to detonate.

413
00:23:08,320 --> 00:23:12,520
They exploded within a predetermined range of the target.

414
00:23:12,620 --> 00:23:14,340
The fuse is kind of like a radar.

415
00:23:14,440 --> 00:23:16,140
It's got a little radio system in it.

416
00:23:16,240 --> 00:23:19,840
It will emit a signal in the air.

417
00:23:19,940 --> 00:23:22,640
And when it bounces back against a hard object,

418
00:23:22,740 --> 00:23:26,280
like an airplane, it explodes.

419
00:23:26,281 --> 00:23:30,580
Today, the benchmark for bullseye accuracy in war

420
00:23:30,680 --> 00:23:32,020
is the smart bomb.

421
00:23:34,420 --> 00:23:37,560
They used laser guidance from other aircraft,

422
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:40,600
you know, a wingman, to point out and paint the target

423
00:23:40,700 --> 00:23:43,660
with a laser signal so that these bombs would follow that

424
00:23:43,760 --> 00:23:44,700
down to the target.

425
00:23:46,640 --> 00:23:50,500
The laser-guided munitions were a tremendous quantum leap

426
00:23:50,600 --> 00:23:52,100
forward in guiding munitions.

427
00:23:52,101 --> 00:23:54,600
but they had problems.

428
00:23:54,700 --> 00:23:56,680
For one thing, they couldn't see through clouds,

429
00:23:56,780 --> 00:23:58,980
or at least the observers or those who were aiming them

430
00:23:59,080 --> 00:24:00,540
couldn't see through the clouds.

431
00:24:00,640 --> 00:24:01,580
The solution?

432
00:24:01,680 --> 00:24:05,420
JDAM, short for Joint Direct Attack Munitions.

433
00:24:05,520 --> 00:24:09,520
It's the epitome of ballistic surgical precision.

434
00:24:09,620 --> 00:24:13,560
Even when the smallest of targets is concealed by cloud cover

435
00:24:13,660 --> 00:24:16,660
or the canopy of night, this smart bomb

436
00:24:16,760 --> 00:24:19,820
uses GPS and inertial navigation tools

437
00:24:19,821 --> 00:24:23,820
GPS and inertial navigation system monitors to zero in

438
00:24:23,920 --> 00:24:24,820
and destroy it.

439
00:24:29,820 --> 00:24:31,560
Hitting the bullseye doesn't always

440
00:24:31,660 --> 00:24:33,560
require such high tech.

441
00:24:33,660 --> 00:24:37,480
All this expert needs is a skilled hand and a girl with

442
00:24:37,580 --> 00:24:38,280
guts.

443
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:46,220
David Adamovich, better known as the great Throdini,

444
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:49,020
is a master of one of the most time-honored and dangerous

445
00:24:49,021 --> 00:24:53,820
and dangerous tests of bullseye accuracy, knife throwing.

446
00:24:53,821 --> 00:24:57,420
His mastery, however, is to miss.

447
00:24:57,421 --> 00:24:59,220
The entertainment value of his art

448
00:24:59,221 --> 00:25:01,780
hinges on David's ability to miss his assistant

449
00:25:01,781 --> 00:25:03,420
by just the slimmest of margins.

450
00:25:06,660 --> 00:25:10,120
Slightest error can have dire consequences.

451
00:25:10,121 --> 00:25:13,680
When I'm throwing, that which goes through my head the most

452
00:25:13,681 --> 00:25:17,880
is staying away from the girl and being in my zone.

453
00:25:17,881 --> 00:25:22,880
My target girl is, in essence, an imaginary blur in the zone

454
00:25:22,881 --> 00:25:26,920
that I don't want to throw to versus my focus on the side of

455
00:25:26,921 --> 00:25:31,400
the board where I am throwing. For greatest accuracy,

456
00:25:31,401 --> 00:25:35,000
impalement artists throw each knife end over end, spinning it

457
00:25:35,001 --> 00:25:38,560
like a pinwheel. The number of revolutions

458
00:25:38,561 --> 00:25:42,280
increases the farther the thrower is from the target.

459
00:25:42,281 --> 00:25:46,500
From around 7 feet, the shortest standard distance, the knife is

460
00:25:46,501 --> 00:25:49,740
by the blade that makes one half spin.

461
00:25:49,840 --> 00:25:52,840
From about 11 feet, the knife is held by the handle

462
00:25:52,940 --> 00:25:56,640
and completes one full spin.

463
00:25:56,740 --> 00:26:00,440
I'm going to show you how I aim to miss at one and a half

464
00:26:00,540 --> 00:26:04,960
spin using target girl Tina.

465
00:26:05,060 --> 00:26:08,060
To successfully complete a one and a half spin throw,

466
00:26:08,160 --> 00:26:12,000
David must move back to a distance of 15 feet.

467
00:26:12,100 --> 00:26:15,560
At this range, the chances for error and the dangers

468
00:26:15,561 --> 00:26:16,560
multiply.

469
00:26:21,300 --> 00:26:24,100
David's not just accurate, but speedy.

470
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:26,640
The Guinness Book of World Records lists him

471
00:26:26,740 --> 00:26:29,400
as the world's fastest knife thrower.

472
00:26:29,500 --> 00:26:34,040
10 knives in 4.2 seconds.

473
00:26:34,140 --> 00:26:35,880
Speed throwing is very difficult.

474
00:26:35,980 --> 00:26:38,580
I'm grabbing knives and throwing knives,

475
00:26:38,680 --> 00:26:43,060
approximately a quarter second each, so that each throw is

476
00:26:43,160 --> 00:26:45,020
about 4 tenths of a second.

477
00:26:45,021 --> 00:26:48,420
So I have to be on my mark perfectly.

478
00:26:48,520 --> 00:26:52,260
I have to grab and throw without ever looking at the knives,

479
00:26:52,360 --> 00:26:56,400
just watching back and forth where I throw the knife.

480
00:26:56,500 --> 00:26:59,400
David's challenge here is to rapidly hurl 10 knives

481
00:26:59,500 --> 00:27:01,700
that will alternate on both the right and left side

482
00:27:01,800 --> 00:27:04,300
of his partner's body.

483
00:27:04,400 --> 00:27:06,300
It's called the Ladder of Death.

484
00:27:15,020 --> 00:27:17,180
I throw anything with a point.

485
00:27:17,280 --> 00:27:20,720
The bigger they are, the further out I have to go.

486
00:27:20,820 --> 00:27:24,320
And when they get big like this and I go that far back,

487
00:27:24,420 --> 00:27:26,940
that is the most dangerous stuff I can do

488
00:27:27,040 --> 00:27:29,060
in the impalement darts.

489
00:27:29,160 --> 00:27:33,680
And by all means, it makes me most scared.

490
00:27:33,780 --> 00:27:35,300
Throwing weapons this dangerous

491
00:27:35,400 --> 00:27:38,340
magnifies the need for bullseye accuracy.

492
00:27:38,440 --> 00:27:41,280
No one knows that better than Tina, his target girl.

493
00:27:41,380 --> 00:27:44,380
I'm fully aware there exists the possibility

494
00:27:44,381 --> 00:27:45,780
of something going wrong.

495
00:27:45,880 --> 00:27:47,620
It's the nature of the act.

496
00:27:47,720 --> 00:27:50,020
You have to commit to it 100%.

497
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:52,880
It can't be halfway.

498
00:27:52,980 --> 00:27:55,720
Go big or go home.

499
00:27:55,820 --> 00:27:57,720
It's now do or die.

500
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:19,380
Just when you think things can't get more dangerous,

501
00:28:19,381 --> 00:28:22,980
David has one more test of his bullseye accuracy

502
00:28:22,981 --> 00:28:26,420
up his sleeve. He will try to sever the straw held

503
00:28:26,421 --> 00:28:28,420
between Tina's teeth.

504
00:28:36,340 --> 00:28:39,380
There may not be the same element of danger in the game

505
00:28:39,381 --> 00:28:44,380
mastered by two-time nine-ball world champion Johnny Archer.

506
00:28:44,381 --> 00:28:47,580
but it requires even more precision,

507
00:28:47,581 --> 00:28:52,080
because each shot requires hitting multiple bullseyes,

508
00:28:52,081 --> 00:28:57,200
from the cue striking the cue ball in exactly the right spot

509
00:28:57,201 --> 00:29:00,100
to the cue ball halting in an advantageous position,

510
00:29:00,101 --> 00:29:03,120
a line for the next shot.

511
00:29:03,121 --> 00:29:05,260
Johnny makes it all look easy, but he

512
00:29:05,261 --> 00:29:07,100
says the hardest shot of all can

513
00:29:07,101 --> 00:29:09,440
be the simple-looking straight long shot.

514
