00:34:07,420 --> 00:34:13,260
to a bullseye on Mars just 70 miles long and 15 miles wide.

601
00:34:13,360 --> 00:34:15,060
The odds were against them.

602
00:34:15,160 --> 00:34:17,920
Of the 13 missions that had tried to land probes on Mars

603
00:34:18,020 --> 00:34:22,000
previously, only five had succeeded.

604
00:34:22,100 --> 00:34:24,640
NASA likened the effort to shooting an arrow from Dodger

605
00:34:24,740 --> 00:34:29,180
Stadium in Los Angeles, traveling 2,077 miles,

606
00:34:29,181 --> 00:34:31,680
hitting home plate at Wrigley Field in Chicago.

607
00:34:35,620 --> 00:34:38,120
NASA's engineers couldn't simply aim Phoenix directly

608
00:34:38,220 --> 00:34:41,520
toward Mars.

609
00:34:41,620 --> 00:34:44,860
Since the red planet orbits the sun at nearly 54,000 miles

610
00:34:44,960 --> 00:34:48,400
per hour, they would have to send their probe toward a point

611
00:34:48,500 --> 00:34:53,940
where they calculated Mars would be 10 months in the future.

612
00:34:54,040 --> 00:34:57,200
If you're shooting skeet, you don't aim where the skeet is

613
00:34:57,201 --> 00:35:00,440
you aim in front of it, because you're not going to hit it

614
00:35:00,540 --> 00:35:01,840
if you shoot where it is now.

615
00:35:01,940 --> 00:35:05,140
You've got to anticipate where it's going.

616
00:35:05,240 --> 00:35:07,840
But engineers knew that the odds of hitting their bullseye

617
00:35:07,940 --> 00:35:10,840
on Mars would be astronomical if they depended solely

618
00:35:10,940 --> 00:35:13,140
on the timing and direction of their launch.

619
00:35:15,660 --> 00:35:18,260
That's because an interplanetary probe, surprisingly,

620
00:35:18,360 --> 00:35:21,560
is at the mercy of countless obstacles in the deceptively

621
00:35:21,660 --> 00:35:24,460
tranquil vacuum of space.

622
00:35:24,461 --> 00:35:28,460
Launching a spacecraft from Earth and having it get from Earth to Mars

623
00:35:28,560 --> 00:35:32,100
is a lot like the game of golf.

624
00:35:32,200 --> 00:35:35,100
There's wind coming from various directions.

625
00:35:35,200 --> 00:35:38,500
There are trees and sand traps along the way too.

626
00:35:38,600 --> 00:35:42,120
So in the case of a spacecraft traveling from Earth to Mars,

627
00:35:42,220 --> 00:35:44,880
we also have winds to contend with.

628
00:35:44,980 --> 00:35:46,880
In this case, it's solar winds

629
00:35:46,980 --> 00:35:49,980
that affect where the spacecraft is traveling.

630
00:35:50,080 --> 00:35:53,860
There's the gravitational forces of the Earth, the moon,

631
00:35:53,861 --> 00:35:58,600
the moon, the asteroid belt beyond the orbit of Mars,

632
00:35:58,700 --> 00:36:02,340
the large planets, Jupiter, Saturn, the sun itself,

633
00:36:02,440 --> 00:36:06,140
are forces acting on the spacecraft almost like winds.

634
00:36:06,240 --> 00:36:08,100
To keep Phoenix on target,

635
00:36:08,200 --> 00:36:10,680
NASA's engineers planned several course corrections

636
00:36:10,780 --> 00:36:13,080
at predetermined times during the voyage,

637
00:36:13,180 --> 00:36:15,920
using thrusters on the spacecraft.

638
00:36:16,020 --> 00:36:18,660
Making those corrections required NASA's engineers

639
00:36:18,760 --> 00:36:23,520
to first know Phoenix's precise location, heading, and speed.

640
00:36:23,521 --> 00:36:26,560
They acquired this data from the deep space network of antennas

641
00:36:26,660 --> 00:36:30,000
situated in California, Spain, and Australia,

642
00:36:30,100 --> 00:36:33,700
which was in constant communication with the spacecraft.

643
00:36:33,800 --> 00:36:38,960
On May 17, 2008, 10 months after it departed Earth,

644
00:36:39,060 --> 00:36:43,300
Phoenix sped within 6 million miles of Mars.

645
00:36:43,400 --> 00:36:46,840
NASA executed its fourth and final course correction,

646
00:36:46,940 --> 00:36:49,420
